WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:05.120
R-rated films are supposed to push your&nbsp;
buttons. With freedom to deliver language,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:09.760
blood, nudity, and drug use, you can&nbsp;
provide lots of provocative material.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.760 --> 00:00:14.800
In some instances, though, R-rated movies use&nbsp;
the creative freedom of their rating for less&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.800 --> 00:00:18.134
savory purposes. These are&nbsp;
the films that go too far.

00:00:18.800 --> 00:00:23.200
Every generation gets their own take on the&nbsp;
classic story The Most Dangerous Game, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.200 --> 00:00:28.800
this generation's incarnation came with The Hunt.&nbsp;
This Craig Zobel film concerns a group of people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:33.840
who wake up and find out they're being hunted&nbsp;
for sport, like any version of this tale does.

00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:37.600
The twist this time, though, was the story&nbsp;
got filtered through the modern-day political&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.600 --> 00:00:42.320
zeitgeist, with the hunters being left-leaning&nbsp;
"elites," while the victims were coded as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.320 --> 00:00:47.440
conservatives. On paper, it's easy to see how the&nbsp;
project received a green-light as something that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.440 --> 00:00:52.240
in the vein of Team America: World Police, could&nbsp;
generate political discourse while drumming up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.240 --> 00:00:57.157
ticket sales from moviegoers who wanted to be&nbsp;
part of the conversation and the dark humor.

00:00:57.157 --> 00:01:01.680
However, the marketing for The Hunt&nbsp;
inspired a wave of unforeseen controversy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:06.240
largely from right-leaning news organizations.&nbsp;
It even garnered an implied response from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:11.120
former President Donald Trump, who accused&nbsp;
Hollywood of merely trying to stir up violence

00:01:11.120 --> 00:01:12.880
The cause of all the brouhaha was that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.880 --> 00:01:16.640
many saw The Hunt as endorsing&nbsp;
violence against conservatives,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.640 --> 00:01:21.217
though this seemed to ignore how the conservative&nbsp;
characters were the heroes of the movie.

00:01:21.217 --> 00:01:25.920
Still, the uproar over The Hunt had a sizable&nbsp;
impact and inspired the film to get delayed a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:30.080
whole six months. When it came time for&nbsp;
this generation's Most Dangerous Game,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.080 --> 00:01:33.394
perhaps the filmmakers tried&nbsp;
to push a few too many buttons.

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:38.640
R-rated comedies can sometimes draw flack&nbsp;
for getting too racy with certain jokes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.640 --> 00:01:43.760
but The Interview inspired a whole other level&nbsp;
of vitriol. The film about two goofballs getting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.760 --> 00:01:48.640
pressed into assassinating Kim Jong-un&nbsp;
drew negative responses from North Korea,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.640 --> 00:01:51.760
especially a scene depicting&nbsp;
the leader's gruesome death.

00:01:51.760 --> 00:01:56.400
In fact, the film was so controversial that&nbsp;
it inspired a hack, apparently stemming from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.400 --> 00:02:01.120
North Korea, that leaked countless pieces&nbsp;
of sensitive information, all stemming from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.120 --> 00:02:06.240
distributor Sony Pictures. Things got even&nbsp;
crazier when the hackers eventually graduated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.240 --> 00:02:10.701
to threatening extreme violence against&nbsp;
movie theaters that screened The Interview.

00:02:10.701 --> 00:02:14.880
The consequences of the film's provocative&nbsp;
humor were swift. By mid-December,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.880 --> 00:02:20.000
the movie's theatrical release was largely&nbsp;
canned, but that wasn't the end of The Interview.

00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:24.960
On the contrary, the movie continued to inspire&nbsp;
widespread discussion on how to respond when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.960 --> 00:02:29.600
violent threats are made against artists. In the&nbsp;
years since its release, The Interview actor and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.600 --> 00:02:34.415
co-director Seth Rogen has been upfront&nbsp;
on what a grueling experience it all was.

00:02:34.415 --> 00:02:36.056
Rogen said to CinemaBlend,

00:02:36.056 --> 00:02:37.797
"Truthfully, after 'The Interview,'&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.797 --> 00:02:41.600
[co-director Evan Goldberg and I]&nbsp;
were a little traumatized, I think."

00:02:41.600 --> 00:02:44.640
Like many of Rogen's comedies, The&nbsp;
Interview was something that could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.640 --> 00:02:47.600
provoke laughs but was also meant to push buttons,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.600 --> 00:02:53.035
but nobody could've expected the outsized and&nbsp;
unprecedented response its material received.

00:02:53.600 --> 00:02:58.080
The anthology comedy Movie 43 provides more&nbsp;
stars than there are in the heavens across&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.080 --> 00:03:02.000
its various individual segments.&nbsp;
Whereas wall-to-wall crudeness in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:05.760
other comedies can help unearth some&nbsp;
kind of deeper truth about humanity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.760 --> 00:03:10.880
Movie 43 is now widely regarded as one of the&nbsp;
worst comedies to ever hit theater screens.

00:03:10.880 --> 00:03:15.680
The participation of movie stars ranging from&nbsp;
Hugh Jackman to Richard Gere to Emma Stone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.680 --> 00:03:20.480
couldn't help elevate the film. The only&nbsp;
conversation drummed up by this box office dud&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.480 --> 00:03:26.080
was a handful of horrific moments that showed the&nbsp;
film's desperation to wring yuks from moviegoers.

00:03:26.080 --> 00:03:32.240
Movie 43 employed yellowface, incest, and a&nbsp;
closing sequence largely focused on physical abuse&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.240 --> 00:03:37.040
being hurled at a character played by&nbsp;
Elizabeth Banks. Movie 43 embraced the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.040 --> 00:03:41.680
idea of overloading the viewer on "shocking"&nbsp;
material. Though the movie pushed the boundaries&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.680 --> 00:03:46.732
of what the R-rating would allow, Movie&nbsp;
43 was hollow vulgarity to its very core.

00:03:47.440 --> 00:03:52.400
Released in 2011, The Hangover Part II drew&nbsp;
some heavy criticism for going way too far&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.400 --> 00:03:57.200
in its attempts to be funny. For example, many&nbsp;
people were outraged when the character of Stu&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.200 --> 00:03:59.420
learns that he slept with a transgender woman.

00:04:00.080 --> 00:04:01.520
“We didn’t get married, did we?”

00:04:01.520 --> 00:04:05.280
“Of course not! We just had some&nbsp;
fun in the chardonnay room!”

00:04:05.280 --> 00:04:09.280
The depiction of this revelation as being&nbsp;
"disgusting" was correctly labeled by many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.280 --> 00:04:14.960
as an example of bro comedies using blatant&nbsp;
transphobia as a substitute for actual comedy.

00:04:14.960 --> 00:04:19.920
Garnering even more media attention at the time&nbsp;
was a photo in the end credits. The image parodies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.920 --> 00:04:25.680
an actual photo taken in the Vietnam War of a&nbsp;
South Vietnamese general executing a prisoner.

00:04:25.680 --> 00:04:28.560
The moment was dubbed by&nbsp;
critics like Roger Ebert as,

00:04:28.560 --> 00:04:32.897
"A desecration of one of [...] the most&nbsp;
famous photos to come out of the Vietnam War."

00:04:32.897 --> 00:04:36.480
Here, fans were disappointed that a movie&nbsp;
that was all about shock value crossed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.480 --> 00:04:40.941
over from providing adult-skewing&nbsp;
gags into outright disrespect.
 

00:04:40.941 --> 00:04:45.520
Zack and Miri Make a Porno is actually a sweet&nbsp;
movie when you get right down to it — one about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.520 --> 00:04:49.200
lifelong best friends who may actually&nbsp;
have romantic feelings for one another.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.200 --> 00:04:52.000
However, the fact that it had&nbsp;
the word "porno" in its title&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.000 --> 00:04:55.115
meant the film already rubbed&nbsp;
some moviegoers the wrong way.

00:04:55.115 --> 00:04:59.680
This was especially true in the movie's marketing,&nbsp;
which kicked off with a poster that proved so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.680 --> 00:05:04.480
suggestive that it ended up getting banned from&nbsp;
being shown in movie theaters. Sometimes, a movie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.480 --> 00:05:09.760
that stirs up this much controversy can ride&nbsp;
all the publicity to sizable box office success.

00:05:09.760 --> 00:05:12.080
Unfortunately, none of these marketing troubles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.080 --> 00:05:17.157
inspired much interest from moviegoers.&nbsp;
Released at the very end of October 2008,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.157 --> 00:05:21.353
Zack and Miri Make a Porno ended up&nbsp;
sinking without a trace at the box office.

00:05:21.353 --> 00:05:24.240
The Kevin Smith comedy, despite&nbsp;
featuring raunchy scenes like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.240 --> 00:05:28.587
Justin Long as an adult film star listing&nbsp;
off the titles of movies he's appeared in,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.587 --> 00:05:31.520
is ultimately a film that wants&nbsp;
to tug at your heartstrings.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.520 --> 00:05:36.289
Too bad its marketing garnered a reputation for&nbsp;
being all the wrong kinds of boundary-pushing.

00:05:36.800 --> 00:05:41.200
Released in 2007, The Hills Have Eyes 2&nbsp;
is the kind of schlocky horror B-movie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.200 --> 00:05:44.800
that audiences tend to watch&nbsp;
because of its tasteless material.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.800 --> 00:05:48.257
Graphic violence, especially,&nbsp;
is the forte of this subgenre.

00:05:48.257 --> 00:05:53.600
However, this particular horror sequel used its&nbsp;
sensibilities not to depict over-the-top violence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.600 --> 00:05:58.000
but to continue a long tradition of using&nbsp;
sexual assault in a throwaway manner. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.000 --> 00:06:02.400
Hills Have Eyes 2 centers much of its&nbsp;
plot on the prospect of deformed mutants&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.400 --> 00:06:05.760
capturing human women for the&nbsp;
purposes of forced breeding.

00:06:05.760 --> 00:06:10.400
The movie's emphasis on this element is made&nbsp;
apparent in an opening scene, one depicting an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.400 --> 00:06:15.440
unnamed woman trapped in the mutants' basement&nbsp;
solely so she can breed children. Before she can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.440 --> 00:06:20.935
establish even the barest personality, she's&nbsp;
slaughtered after birthing a stillborn baby.

00:06:20.935 --> 00:06:23.440
Critics like Scott Tobias took The Hills Have Eyes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.440 --> 00:06:27.520
2 to task for resorting to such a&nbsp;
shallow depiction of sexual assault,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.520 --> 00:06:31.494
as well as for not giving any of the women&nbsp;
in the film basic character development.

00:06:31.840 --> 00:06:37.200
While some grindhouse movies like Abel Ferrara's&nbsp;
Ms. 45 have garnered praise for using grimy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.200 --> 00:06:41.920
exploitation cinema to thoughtfully explore&nbsp;
the perspective of a sexual assault survivor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.920 --> 00:06:46.914
the tactless treatment of sexual assault in&nbsp;
The Hills Have Eyes 2 is just plain appalling.

00:06:47.440 --> 00:06:53.360
The 2007 Uwe Boll movie Postal opens with&nbsp;
a sequence depicting Flight 11 just moments&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.360 --> 00:06:58.800
before it hit the North Tower, beginning the 9/11&nbsp;
terrorist attacks. In this version of the event,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.800 --> 00:07:03.600
the two hijackers piloting the plane decide to&nbsp;
abort the mission... only for the passengers to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.600 --> 00:07:08.080
break into the cockpit and inadvertently&nbsp;
cause the plane to crash into the tower.

00:07:08.080 --> 00:07:11.600
A sequence like this was always meant&nbsp;
to establish the tasteless - and,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.600 --> 00:07:15.600
if the general critical reception is any&nbsp;
indicator, humorless - aesthetic of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.600 --> 00:07:20.800
movie ahead. But the film's opening gag was&nbsp;
perceived by many as way too provocative,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.800 --> 00:07:24.960
to the point that one of its actors believed&nbsp;
it cost the film its commercial potential.

00:07:24.960 --> 00:07:29.360
In the book My Year of Flops by Nathan&nbsp;
Rabin , performer Dave Foley remarked,

00:07:29.360 --> 00:07:33.360
"I think that crashing a plane into the Twin&nbsp;
Towers at the start of the film hurt it."

00:07:33.360 --> 00:07:37.600
Postal, like its video game source&nbsp;
material, wanted to shock and offend,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.600 --> 00:07:41.600
but it seems that it went above and beyond&nbsp;
the call of duty in its opening sequence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.600 --> 00:07:44.875
which took things from vulgar&nbsp;
comedy to just plain vulgar.

00:07:45.520 --> 00:07:50.800
This Cuba Gooding Jr. and Horatio Sanz story&nbsp;
concerns two men who enlist in a singles cruise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.800 --> 00:07:54.400
only to discover it's exclusively&nbsp;
for gay men. They then pose as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.400 --> 00:07:57.280
gay while trying to get closer&nbsp;
to women who work on the boat.

00:07:57.280 --> 00:08:02.080
The comedy is full of jokes about straight men&nbsp;
being terrified of the very presence of gay people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.080 --> 00:08:06.240
and how "gross" the thought of two dudes&nbsp;
kissing is. Critics savaged the film for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.240 --> 00:08:11.600
its rampant homophobia, though many writers&nbsp;
also slammed the movie for being too tedious.

00:08:11.600 --> 00:08:16.080
Rita Kempley of The Washington Post, for&nbsp;
example, remarked that the tired and familiar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.080 --> 00:08:21.040
attempts at comedy were just as insulting as&nbsp;
the rampant gay panic material, adding that,

00:08:21.040 --> 00:08:24.895
"It's time many of the movie's more offensive&nbsp;
gags […] went back into the closet."

00:08:25.440 --> 00:08:29.760
Few films of the 1990s were as&nbsp;
controversial as Natural Born Killers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.760 --> 00:08:34.560
the tale of murderous lovers Mickey and Mallory,&nbsp;
played by Woody Harrelson and Juliette Lewis.

00:08:34.560 --> 00:08:38.400
Intended by writer and director Oliver&nbsp;
Stone as a condemnation of the heavy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.400 --> 00:08:43.120
presence of violence in the media, the general&nbsp;
response to the movie is still divided today&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.120 --> 00:08:46.497
over whether or not the film&nbsp;
achieved its intended goals.

00:08:46.497 --> 00:08:49.840
Critic Janet Maslin, for instance,&nbsp;
observed that the movie seems to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.840 --> 00:08:52.880
be more in love with graphic&nbsp;
violence than critical of it.

00:08:52.880 --> 00:08:54.075
Maslin observed,

00:08:54.075 --> 00:08:58.880
"While 'Natural Born Killers' affects occasional&nbsp;
disgust at the lurid world of Mickey and Mallory,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.880 --> 00:09:02.080
it more often seems enamored&nbsp;
of their exhilarating freedom."

00:09:02.080 --> 00:09:05.680
Critics who interpreted the film as being&nbsp;
more of a love letter to this kind of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.680 --> 00:09:11.200
behavior saw Natural Born Killers as a lot of&nbsp;
ultra-violent noise without much of a purpose.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.200 --> 00:09:15.280
Similarly, much has been written about how&nbsp;
the film's treatment of indigenous cultures&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.280 --> 00:09:19.440
is almost as stomach-churning as its&nbsp;
most gruesome instance of violence.

00:09:19.440 --> 00:09:24.160
With this 1994 film, Stone meant to make&nbsp;
something that inspired a reaction in people.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.160 --> 00:09:27.840
He succeeded, but unfortunately, it&nbsp;
inspired a whole lot of critiques that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.840 --> 00:09:32.694
Stone had made something as indulgent as the&nbsp;
elements of society he intended to condemn.

00:09:33.520 --> 00:09:37.200
“You’ll never understand, Wayne.&nbsp;
You and me? We’re not even the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.200 --> 00:09:39.920
species. I used to be you, but I’ve evolved.”

00:09:40.640 --> 00:09:44.480
Revenge of the Nerds has often been seen&nbsp;
as a film championing the downtrodden,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.480 --> 00:09:49.120
a comedic form of voyeurism wherein the oppressed&nbsp;
could live out their greatest fantasies.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.120 --> 00:09:53.360
Now for many, Revenge of the Nerds&nbsp;
is seen as trivializing rape culture.

00:09:53.360 --> 00:09:56.800
This is most apparent in an infamous&nbsp;
sequence where the character of Lewis&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.800 --> 00:10:01.200
pretends to be the jock Stan so he can sleep&nbsp;
with the quarterback's girlfriend, Betty.

00:10:01.200 --> 00:10:04.240
It's meant to be a cheer-worthy&nbsp;
sequence in the movie itself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.240 --> 00:10:08.000
but it registers to many now as&nbsp;
an endorsement of sexual assault.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.000 --> 00:10:13.520
Director Jeff Kanew himself has recently expressed&nbsp;
regret over ever including the scene in the film.

00:10:13.520 --> 00:10:15.600
In 2019, Kanew told GQ,

00:10:16.320 --> 00:10:20.000
"I've heard [criticism] a lot this year&nbsp;
because of the #MeToo movement. [...] At&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.000 --> 00:10:23.360
the time, it was considered sort of&nbsp;
a switch. [...] It's not excusable.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.360 --> 00:10:26.240
If it were my daughter, I&nbsp;
probably wouldn't like it."

00:10:26.240 --> 00:10:31.040
The R-rated events of Revenge of the Nerds were&nbsp;
intended to be seen as conventional escapism&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.040 --> 00:10:35.000
but it's apparent to many now that the&nbsp;
movie is more insidious than humorous.

00:10:35.520 --> 00:10:40.800
In his voyage up the river to find Marlon Brando's&nbsp;
Colonel Kurtz, Martin Sheen's Captain Willard sees&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:40.800 --> 00:10:46.240
a wide array of horrific and unforgettable sights&nbsp;
that lay the horrors of the Vietnam War bare.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.240 --> 00:10:50.456
Unfortunately, viewers are also exposed&nbsp;
to a terrible sight late in the film.

00:10:50.456 --> 00:10:55.840
For a climactic scene involving a ritual performed&nbsp;
by an Ifugao tribe, a water buffalo is killed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.840 --> 00:11:00.320
on-screen. This was apparently a real-life&nbsp;
practice that was already underway before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.320 --> 00:11:05.920
cameras started rolling, with director Francis&nbsp;
Ford Coppola filming the ritual as it occurred.

00:11:05.920 --> 00:11:08.960
While intended to be a visual&nbsp;
parallel to Willard's plan to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:08.960 --> 00:11:12.880
assassinate Kurtz with a machete, the&nbsp;
sight of an animal killed on-screen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:12.880 --> 00:11:16.320
is jarring enough to render that&nbsp;
thematic undercurrent irrelevant.

00:11:16.320 --> 00:11:19.760
Decades after the film's release,&nbsp;
Coppola has stood by the scene,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:19.760 --> 00:11:23.040
reaffirming that he was merely&nbsp;
documenting reality itself.

00:11:23.040 --> 00:11:25.040
Coppola said to USA Today,

00:11:25.040 --> 00:11:28.400
"I did not direct it or anything,&nbsp;
that was the way they do it."

00:11:28.400 --> 00:11:32.480
To prove his point, Coppola made sure to&nbsp;
emphasize that he refused to have an extra&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:32.480 --> 00:11:38.240
water buffalo around to kill in case extra takes&nbsp;
were needed. Despite Coppola's statements, this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:38.240 --> 00:11:43.280
scene of animal cruelty stands out in Apocalypse&nbsp;
Now as horrifying for all the wrong reasons.

